## 5. Forms (Signal Forms Now Standard)

### BREAKING CHANGE: Reactive Forms → Signal Forms (2026)

**Reactive Forms are DEPRECATED for new code in 2026. Use Signal Forms instead.**

#### What is Signal Forms?

Signal Forms combine signals + ngModel for compile-time type safety, zero boilerplate, and zoneless compatibility.

```typescript
// ❌ OLD WAY (Reactive Forms, now legacy):
@Component({
  template: `
    <input [formControl]="nameControl">
    {{ nameControl.value }}
  `
})
export class OldFormComponent {
  nameControl = new FormControl('John');
}

// ✅ NEW WAY (Signal Forms, 2026 standard):
@Component({
  template: `
    <input [(ngModel)]="name">
    {{ name() }}
  `
})
export class NewFormComponent {
  name = model('John'); // Signal + ngModel built-in!
}

// model() is:
// - signal() with automatic ngModel binding
// - Type-safe: model<User>() knows exact type
// - Zero boilerplate: no FormBuilder needed
// - Zoneless compatible: works perfectly with new change detection
```

#### Real-World Signal Form Example

```typescript
interface User {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
  email: string;
}

// ❌ OLD REACTIVE FORMS PATTERN (Deprecated 2026)
@Component({
  template: `
    <form [formGroup]="form" (ngSubmit)="submit()">
      <input formControlName="firstName">
      <input formControlName="lastName">
      <input formControlName="email">
      <button>Save</button>
    </form>
    {{ form.valid }}
  `
})
export class OldFormComponent {
  form = this.fb.group({
    firstName: ['', Validators.required],
    lastName: ['', Validators.required],
    email: ['', [Validators.required, Validators.email]]
  });
  
  constructor(private fb: FormBuilder) {}
  
  submit() {
    if (this.form.valid) {
      console.log(this.form.value);
    }
  }
}

// ✅ NEW SIGNAL FORMS PATTERN (2026 Standard)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-user-form',
  template: `
    <form (ngSubmit)="submit()">
      <input [(ngModel)]="user().firstName" type="text">
      <input [(ngModel)]="user().lastName" type="text">
      <input [(ngModel)]="user().email" type="email">
      
      <p *ngIf="!isValid()">Form invalid</p>
      <button [disabled]="!isValid()">Save</button>
    </form>
  `
})
export class UserFormComponent {
  user = model<User>({ 
    firstName: '', 
    lastName: '', 
    email: '' 
  });
  
  // Computed validation
  isValid = computed(() => {
    const u = this.user();
    return u.firstName.length > 0 &&
           u.lastName.length > 0 &&
           u.email.includes('@');
  });
  
  isDirty = signal(false);
  
  submit() {
    if (this.isValid()) {
      console.log(this.user());
    }
  }
}

// 🏆 BEST PRACTICE: Separate logic from UI
@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class UserFormService {
  user = signal<User>({ firstName: '', lastName: '', email: '' });
  
  isValid = computed(() => {
    const u = this.user();
    return u.firstName.length > 0 &&
           u.lastName.length > 0 &&
           u.email.includes('@');
  });
  
  updateField(field: keyof User, value: string) {
    this.user.update(current => ({
      ...current,
      [field]: value
    }));
  }
  
  save() {
    if (this.isValid()) {
      return this.http.post('/api/users', this.user());
    }
  }
}

@Component({
  template: `
    <input [(ngModel)]="user().firstName" 
           (ngModelChange)="updateField('firstName', $event)">
    <p *ngIf="!isValid()">Form invalid</p>
    <button (click)="save()">Save</button>
  `
})
export class UserFormComponent {
  user = this.service.user;
  isValid = this.service.isValid;
  
  constructor(readonly service: UserFormService) {}
  
  updateField(field: keyof User, value: string) {
    this.service.updateField(field, value);
  }
  
  save() {
    this.service.save().subscribe();
  }
}
```

#### Why Signal Forms Matter

1. **Type Safety**: `model<User>()` prevents runtime type errors
2. **Zero Boilerplate**: No FormBuilder, FormGroup, FormControl setup
3. **Zoneless Compatible**: Works perfectly with new change detection
4. **Automatic Optimization**: Only affected signals trigger re-renders
5. **Same Everywhere**: Uses signals pattern throughout your app

**Interview Answer:** "Signal Forms are the 2026 standard, replacing Reactive Forms. They eliminate boilerplate, add compile-time type safety, and optimize automatically for zoneless change detection. For any new project, Signal Forms are the go-to choice."

